triage: fix_tests: The failing test checks the length of `lot_list`. That attribute is not in the interface: it is the coder's own scratch value, emptied while shuffling the lots into `lots`, which holds the 11 lots not yet used after round 1 was dealt. Everything the interface does define came out as the rules say: seat 1 kept B1, G4 went to auction, both bids were 0, the auction card went back to seat 1, all gold stayed at 12 and the worths stayed at 3.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: revise_rules: The game behaves as specified, yet a thoughtless policy dominates. Always taking the first option (keep the first card, bid 0) wins about 80-85% of matches, and always taking the last option (bid all your gold) almost always loses. Bidding is a losing proposition: the winner's gold goes straight to a rival, gold converts directly to VP, and tied or zero bids hand the card to the seller for free.
